WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.139 --> 00:00:04.679
From there, let's take a look at the object life cycle.

2
00:00:04.679 --> 00:00:06.040
Creating a new object in Unreal

3
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.679
is as easy as calling the new object line

4
00:00:08.679 --> 00:00:10.900
with the appropriate parameters.

5
00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.380
When we actually create the object,

6
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:15.640
the following operations are going on under the hood.

7
00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:18.320
We malloc the raw data, finding out how big it is

8
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.879
and allocating the associated memory footprint for it.

9
00:00:20.879 --> 00:00:22.160
We then call memset zero

10
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:23.920
and clear all the associated memory

11
00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.859
because we're about to populate it.

12
00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:27.640
We then call the classes constructor

13
00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.120
that initializes the virtual table

14
00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.600
and initializes all properties within that block of memory.

15
00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:35.219
From there, we copy over the archetype with a mem copy

16
00:00:35.219 --> 00:00:36.840
and Unreal works from a template system

17
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.850
called the class default object.

18
00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:42.289
We then have the post construction initialization,

19
00:00:42.289 --> 00:00:45.289
which is where we duplicate all values from the CDO

20
00:00:45.289 --> 00:00:47.359
into this new block of data.

21
00:00:47.359 --> 00:00:49.000
From there, we load all properties

22
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:50.640
from configuration files.

23
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.079
We load all properties that would require from .config

24
00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:56.759
or .ini files through a get defaults call.

25
00:00:56.759 --> 00:00:58.640
Lastly, we call post init properties,

26
00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:00.799
which is a gameplay function that we can implement

27
00:01:00.799 --> 00:01:03.310
and use to drive gameplay logic.

28
00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:05.230
Actors have a very specific life cycle

29
00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:07.989
that we can absolutely use to our advantage.

30
00:01:07.989 --> 00:01:10.030
Actors have a huge number of virtual functions

31
00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:12.269
that we can override to add gameplay functionality.

32
00:01:12.269 --> 00:01:14.189
Let's take a look how we can spawn an actor

33
00:01:14.189 --> 00:01:16.859
and break down its subsequent life cycle.

34
00:01:16.859 --> 00:01:18.219
As an actor is in the world,

35
00:01:18.219 --> 00:01:19.379
we need to route our spawn

36
00:01:19.379 --> 00:01:21.299
through the world we wish to spawn it into

37
00:01:21.299 --> 00:01:23.420
and give it a transform to spawn at.

38
00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:25.659
So in this example, we've got a transform defined.

39
00:01:25.659 --> 00:01:28.140
In this case, we'll just use its default values.

40
00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:29.500
We then grab a reference to the world

41
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:30.680
we want to spawn the actor in

42
00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.849
and call its template its spawn actor function.

43
00:01:33.849 --> 00:01:36.090
We give it a template, in this case, just actor,

44
00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.890
and pass it in a class that we'd like to spawn

45
00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:40.819
and a transform that we'd want it to spawn at.

46
00:01:40.819 --> 00:01:42.540
As mentioned on the opening slide,

47
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.480
actors have got a number of critical functions

48
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:45.620
that are important for us to understand

49
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.659
when building systems.

50
00:01:47.659 --> 00:01:49.620
Initialize components pre and post

51
00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:51.379
is called when spinning up the actor.

52
00:01:51.379 --> 00:01:53.060
This function is passed through to any components

53
00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:56.780
that are on the set actor for their own initializations.

54
00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.060
Begin play is called

55
00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.219
as soon as the actor is spawned and is usable.

56
00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:03.579
It's essentially finished its initialization at this point.

57
00:02:03.579 --> 00:02:05.280
Tick is called at regular intervals

58
00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.379
with every frame being its default,

59
00:02:07.379 --> 00:02:10.449
with delta time being passed in as a parameter.

60
00:02:10.449 --> 00:02:12.770
The length of a frame may vary wildly

61
00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:14.169
on a per client basis.

62
00:02:14.169 --> 00:02:17.539
So using delta time in our calculations is fairly critical.

63
00:02:17.539 --> 00:02:20.159
Frame rates can be locked on both client and server

64
00:02:20.159 --> 00:02:22.719
with the tick frequency changing accordingly.

65
00:02:22.719 --> 00:02:25.580
However, we can also explicitly define our tick frequency

66
00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:27.300
in the constructor of the class

67
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.419
should we want a very infrequent but consistent tick.

68
00:02:30.419 --> 00:02:33.060
A tick can also be turned off and unregistered entirely

69
00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.439
if your technical design doesn't require it.

70
00:02:36.439 --> 00:02:38.759
End play is the actor's spin down function

71
00:02:38.759 --> 00:02:40.580
and it's the opposite of begin play.

72
00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:43.699
Typically this is used as a cleanup function.

73
00:02:43.699 --> 00:02:46.939
Calling destroy on an actor marks it as pending kill

74
00:02:46.939 --> 00:02:49.699
and begins the process of destroying the actor.

75
00:02:49.699 --> 00:02:52.620
This involves calling begin destroy, finish destroy

76
00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:55.000
and finally freeing up the memory.

77
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.960
Destroy is often called by engineers

78
00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.400
and it's eventually rooted down into end play.

79
00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.620
So we don't have to call that manually.

80
00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.780
It's important to note that we can still access the actor

81
00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.900
whilst it's in the process of being destroyed

82
00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:10.120
but it will have the RF pending kill flag set on it.

83
00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:11.800
We can see here that the actor life cycle

84
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:13.280
is actually fairly complex

85
00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:14.360
and there's a number of different ways

86
00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.300
we can initialize an actor.

87
00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:17.580
We can see on the right hand side

88
00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.379
that the actual spawning can be instant or deferred.

89
00:03:20.379 --> 00:03:22.259
Deferred gives us a chance to change a value

90
00:03:22.259 --> 00:03:24.500
on said actor before it hits begin play

91
00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:27.569
and any subsequent gameplay functions that we've hooked in.

92
00:03:27.569 --> 00:03:29.449
We can also see on the left that loading a map

93
00:03:29.449 --> 00:03:32.210
also routes through the same begin play code

94
00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:33.969
as our explicit spawning does,

95
00:03:33.969 --> 00:03:35.849
whether it be from the direct loading of a map

96
00:03:35.849 --> 00:03:38.449
or from clicking play in the editor.

97
00:03:38.449 --> 00:03:40.449
We can also see that when and where

98
00:03:40.449 --> 00:03:43.370
end play or destroy is called is also fairly fluid.

99
00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:44.689
We can call destroy ourselves,

100
00:03:44.689 --> 00:03:46.449
we can give an actor a lifetime

101
00:03:46.449 --> 00:03:49.009
or we can simply exit the application.

102
00:03:49.009 --> 00:03:50.289
Regardless, we need to make sure

103
00:03:50.289 --> 00:03:53.009
that we're not leaving any actors unaccounted for.

104
00:03:53.009 --> 00:03:54.930
They all route through end play functionality

105
00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:57.969
marking them for kill and the eventual garbage collection.
